WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:12.260
On the 1st of July this year, Canada celebrated
its 150th birthday.

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:16.360
Now, we told you all about this in a previous
video where we also showed you some of the

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:25.900
keys as to why Canada is one of the most admired
countries in the world.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:36.550
However, even though this country has turned
150 in perfect shape, Canada is also facing

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:40.470
problems: They face social, political, and
economic threats that could be considered…

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.360
well, pretty serious indeed.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.150
As you probably already know, unfortunately
nothing is perfect.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:50.920
So, in a previous video, whose link I´ll
leave at the end of this video as well as

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:55.700
in the description below, we looked on the
bright side of things, and Canada ended up

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.320
looking pretty darn wonderful…

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:02.320
But in this video we are going to discuss
the main problems and threats that are affecting

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.320
Canada.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.810
And the first of them has a lot to do with
this 150th anniversary…

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:16.170
A 
BIRTHDAY SOMEHOW ARTIFICIAL?

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:22.630
Not all Canadians, not even close, celebrated
this 150th birthday.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:26.760
We can find several different reasons why,
but in order to explain them we should first

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.350
tell you about what happened 150 years ago.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:35.140
So let’s take a look back in time, back
to 1867.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.350
On the first of July of that year, the territory
was still part of the British Empire.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:45.990
But it was on this day that the first “British
North America Act” was signed.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:51.490
The “British North America Act of 1867 was
a law that united the three colonial provinces

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:56.520
of New Brunswick, New Scotland and Canada
- which was divided up into Ontario and Quebec

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:01.710
that very same day - into the Confederation
of Canada.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:05.970
On top of these four provinces, which still
kept quite a lot of autonomy, a federal government

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:08.660
was organized to give them some cohesion.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:13.020
And above this government, there were still
the British authorities.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:20.419
In fact, to this day, Canada is still a monarchy
and Queen Elizabeth II is still the head of

00:02:20.419 --> 00:02:21.419
state.

00:02:21.419 --> 00:02:25.010
Also, and although it might sound surprising,
Canada didn’t have total independence from

00:02:25.010 --> 00:02:27.260
the UK until very recently - 1982.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:33.510
Yep, that’s right, up until 1982, any constitutional
modification required the approval of the

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:34.510
British parliament…

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:42.090
So, as you can see, that law in 1867 didn’t
result in a completely independent country.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.950
But, dear viewers, this is not the end of
it.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.779
There are more reasons why many Canadians
dislike this day.

00:02:48.779 --> 00:03:01.319
The most important of all is that, when the
settlers arrived, Canada wasn't an empty land...

00:03:01.319 --> 00:03:06.999
THE FIRST NATIONS, THE INUITS AND THE MÉTIS.

00:03:06.999 --> 00:03:11.670
Around 1.5 million inhabitants had nothing
to celebrate last July… and those inhabitants

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.879
were the natives of Canada.

00:03:13.879 --> 00:03:19.060
“It’s a celebration of the worst 150 years
of indigenous peoples’ lives.”

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.622
Pam Palmater, a lawyer and university professor.

00:03:20.622 --> 00:03:22.809
But don’t think of this as only a historical
problem.

00:03:22.809 --> 00:03:28.069
The recognition and integration of these peoples
continues, to this day, to be a national problem.

00:03:28.069 --> 00:03:32.029
In fact, the government of Justin Trudeau
has made it a priority.

00:03:32.029 --> 00:03:35.790
Consider what the Prime Minister had to say
on the 21st of June this year:

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:41.170
“No relationship is more important to Canada
than the relationship with Indigenous Peoples.”

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.170
Justin Trudeau, Prime Minister of Canada.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:43.170
But…

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.069
now you might be wondering, who are these
groups?

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:51.220
And what has happened to them over the last
150 years?

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:57.260
(According to the Canadian Constitution there
are three large groups: the First Nations

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:03.159
made of more than 600 native communities;
the Inuits: the people of the Arctic; and

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:08.219
the Metis: the result of mixing natives and
the new settlers).

00:04:08.219 --> 00:04:11.150
So Canada was not this empty land when the
settlers arrived...

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.349
It is actually estimated that there were around
500,000 people from these native communities

00:04:15.349 --> 00:04:18.950
who signed agreements with the British and
French authorities.

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:23.130
They even participated in the battles that
protected the independence of Canada.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:28.120
“Canada would be American today if not for
the Indian allies who fought for the Crown”.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:29.120
Peter Russell, a historian.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.580
Oh yes, the US really did try to invade Canada
more than once…

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:35.810
Well, the problem is that, despite being the
first people in this land, and despite the

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:40.400
fact that they participated and contributed
to the independence of the country, the natives

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:46.000
suffered clear cultural persecution.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:53.100
Yes, as a result of that obsession some have
to create homogeneous national identities

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:56.290
Canada went through a period of persecuting
the natives…

00:04:56.290 --> 00:05:00.600
For years, these people suffered the occupation
of their territories and were sent away to

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.650
reservations, which sometimes didn’t even
have the basic conditions that would guarantee

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:06.650
their survival.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:10.570
Furthermore, both their languages and religious
and cultural rituals were sometimes forbidden.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:14.950
This persecution reached such a point that
the authorities set up a system of residences

00:05:14.950 --> 00:05:18.540
and boarding schools for the children of native
families.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:22.100
The government took children from their families
and sent them to schools where they would

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:27.680
receive an education which was fully “western”
so they would lose all aspects of their cultural

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:28.790
identity.

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:33.520
Tens of thousands of children were educated
in this way…

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:42.840
And these schools continued until the 1970s
and the consequences still have to be dealt

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.590
with today: poverty rates were much higher
than average, there are many broken families,

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:53.170
depression is rampant, and alcoholism and
suicide rates are higher than average…

00:05:53.170 --> 00:06:00.960
These are the consequences of this sort of
persecution.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.961
Along with these, racism and exclusion were
also very normal…

00:06:03.961 --> 00:06:12.040
So, in 2008, the Prime Minister at the time,
Stephen Harper, apologized to these peoples

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:30.500
for the atrocities and established the Truth
and Reconciliation Commision.

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:34.100
They have also paid hundreds of millions of
dollars in reparations...

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.550
But money can’t fix all these problems.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:40.670
Even today Canada is still looking for the
best way to move on from the dark period of

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:42.000
its history.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.760
But now, let’s step forward to the present
day and look at the relationship between Canada

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.600
and the US, or more aptly, Canada and Trump…

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:57.900
THE 
BREAK-UP THREAT

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.420
Canada is very dependent on its relationship
with the US.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:05.550
They are so dependent that we could almost
say the Canadians would be unable to live

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.240
without their Southern neighbour.

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:12.240
Indeed, almost 70% of Canadian trade is bound
for the United States.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:16.030
Further, the industrial sector of both countries
is strongly interconnected.

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:18.450
So, this is a special relationship.

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:20.950
And this is something Justin Trudeau knows.

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:25.740
Indeed, he said so in his campaign, and as
soon as he reached power he concentrated his

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:30.760
international affairs into improving Canada’s
relationship with the United States.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:40.160
And truth be told, the relationship he built
with Obama was great.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:08:18.940
But, now Trump is in office and is pursuing
a policy of protectionism…

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:24.650
And what did the Canadian government do?

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:29.150
Well, they set up a sort of “war room”
- an office dedicated to following all the

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:32.019
movements of the new administration in Washington...

00:08:32.019 --> 00:08:35.659
At first, the strategy of Trudeau and his
people was buttering up the American government,

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:37.380
and it looked like it worked.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:41.659
The Canadian Prime Minister even started a
friendship with Ivanka Trump, Trump’s daughter.

00:08:41.659 --> 00:08:46.440
“Justin Trudeau Brings Ivanka Trump to Broadway
Show on Welcoming Outsiders” New York Times.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:47.440
And you can imagine…

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.430
this opened the door to all sort of wild rumours
and speculation.

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:55.559
The thing is, they got on well, and both led
a seminar on women in business that Donald

00:08:55.559 --> 00:09:00.819
Trump himself mentioned in a speech to Congress:
“With the help of Prime Minister Justin

00:09:00.819 --> 00:09:04.879
Trudeau, we have formed a council with our
neighbours in Canada to help ensure that women

00:09:04.879 --> 00:09:09.519
entrepreneurs have access to the networks,
markets and capital they need to start a business

00:09:09.519 --> 00:09:12.520
and live out their financial dreams”.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:13.520
Donald Trump.

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:16.920
Well, it all seemed to work well… but wait
just a second!

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:22.350
Because all of a sudden Trump attacked and
accused Canada rather unexpectedly of unfair

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:23.499
competition...

00:09:23.499 --> 00:09:26.709
something he also did with Mexico.

00:09:26.709 --> 00:09:32.779
It would be dangerous for Canada to suffer
a restriction on trade relations with the

00:09:32.779 --> 00:09:37.519
US so the government in Ottawa decided to
start the so-called “doughnut strategy,”

00:09:37.519 --> 00:09:42.079
consisting of influencing all institutions
around the White House - which would be considered

00:09:42.079 --> 00:09:44.639
being in the hole of the doughnut.

00:09:44.639 --> 00:09:49.410
But the question now becomes, will they achieve
their goal?

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:50.410
Only time will say.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:53.779
The first challenge is to renegotiate NAFTA
as Trump has demanded…

00:09:53.779 --> 00:09:57.970
Now, next up we need to consider the another
potential crisis that could threaten Canadian

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:02.059
prosperity...

00:10:02.059 --> 00:10:03.139
THE REAL-ESTATE BUBBLE

00:10:03.139 --> 00:10:08.380
The prices in Canada’s housing market have
skyrocketed.

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:13.690
They have increased 76% and so have the loans
that banks are providing.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:18.139
Indeed, family debt in Canada is now among
the highest in the world.

00:10:18.139 --> 00:10:25.490
Have a look at this chart to see the increase
in Canadian real-estate prices:

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:32.220
And then there is family debt:

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:35.329
As you might imagine, this has set off all
sorts of economic alarms.

00:10:35.329 --> 00:10:41.230
Many analysts foresee the popping of the Canadian
real-estate bubble, along with all the consequences

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:43.310
that would have on the economy.

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:47.360
And, look, almost 7% of the country's employment
belongs to the construction sector and thanks

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:52.170
to this mortgage boom three Canadian banks
are now in the 20 top banks worldwide.

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:56.990
In fact, the market value of the Royal Bank
of Canada is higher than that of any in the

00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:00.029
Euro zone.

00:11:00.029 --> 00:11:02.059
&nbsp;
As everyone knows, the consequences of the

00:11:02.059 --> 00:11:06.279
real-estate bubble bursting would be pretty
dire, so doing something about this has become

00:11:06.279 --> 00:11:08.819
another priority of the Canadian government.

00:11:08.819 --> 00:11:12.910
The government is trying to stimulate other
sectors, while cooling down the real-estate

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:13.910
market.

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:17.880
Maybe, the intention to increase the arrival
of immigrants by a 50% every year has a lot

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:19.079
to do with this.

00:11:19.079 --> 00:11:21.629
More immigrants means more demand for real
estate.

00:11:21.629 --> 00:11:26.529
But other experts disagree and state that
Canadian banks were not affected by the 2008

00:11:26.529 --> 00:11:31.120
crisis, and that they are quite a bit more
solid than other international banks.

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:35.930
And also, the economy in Canada is doing quite
well and is gaining strength after some pretty

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:39.300
mediocre years.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:45.420
Anyway, time will tell, but this is a risk
the government is going to have to look out

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:46.420
for.

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:53.459
Alright, so let’s now move onto the matter
of public services.

00:11:53.459 --> 00:11:54.459
THE WAITING LISTS

00:11:54.459 --> 00:12:00.759
In Canada, health is a public service which
is managed and financed by the government…

00:12:00.759 --> 00:12:03.110
with the citizens taxes, of course.

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:05.860
The system is considered to be one of the
best in the world, however…

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:09.170
they have recently had to deal with some serious
problems.

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:13.540
The most important of all are waiting lists.

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:18.649
According to the Fraser Institute the average
time to see a specialist is 18 weeks… that’s

00:12:18.649 --> 00:12:19.950
more than 4 months!

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:24.490
And the demographic ageing and the increase
of life expectancy has made it more difficult

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:26.379
for the system to cope.

00:12:26.379 --> 00:12:32.040
This has all resulted in tens of thousands
of Canadians moving to the US to receive medical

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:38.119
attention and, lately, it is even popular
to take out medical insurance in the US.

00:12:38.119 --> 00:12:43.779
Anyway, these are the main challenges and
threats to the government of Justin Trudeau.

00:12:43.779 --> 00:12:44.970
Like we said earlier…

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:47.929
not everything can be sunshine, lollipops
and rainbows.

00:12:47.929 --> 00:12:53.929
But, you know what, the overall result of
the Canadian cocktail looks pretty good!

00:12:53.929 --> 00:12:55.420
So, now it is your turn!

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:57.839
What do you think of Canada and their systems?

00:12:57.839 --> 00:12:59.850
Let us know in the comments below.

